    "body": "The Stibnite gold-antimony project in central Idaho. ( Image: Amanda Stutt, MINING.COM. )\nPosted By: Mining.com Staff November 17, 2026\nPerpetua Resources (Nasdaq, TSX: PPTA) signed a non-binding agreement with an unnamed aerospace and defence company based in the United States to explore funding for a potential tungsten project at Stibnite in central Idaho.\nThe memorandum of understanding contemplates up to ($55.5 million C$79 million) in non-equity funding at the prospective partner’s discretion and a possible right for it to buy up to half of Perpetua’s future tungsten output if production goes ahead. The proposed funding falls under the company’s Industrial Tungsten Acceleration Program (I-TAP).\nEarly discussions with U.S. manufacturers could help Perpetua assess the material, how to process it and how to develop the deposit, president and CEO Jon Cherry said in a company statement .\nPerpetua is assessing whether mineralization in the historic Stibnite Mining District and on nearby claims could support commercial tungsten production. In October, the company announced up to US$40 million in U.S. Army funding to assess whether the district could produce tungsten concentrate.\nThe United States has had no commercial tungsten mining since 2015, according to the U.S. Geological Survey.\n“Stibnite offers an opportunity to evaluate a potential American source of tungsten,” Cherry said.\nShares in Perpetua Resources rose 0.9% to $17.16 Tuesday in Nasdaq trading, valuing the company at about $2.1 billion.\nFuture planning\nAny tungsten development would proceed separately from the approved gold-antimony mine plan and require its own environmental review and permits. Perpetua said it has yet to establish whether the tungsten could be mined commercially.\nIf an agreement is reached and the tungsten program is funded, Perpetua expects initial work to include exploration drilling, resource definition, metallurgical and processing testing, and design and permitting for a sloping underground access tunnel.\nA potential second phase could include underground exploration and development, a preliminary economic evaluation, pilot-scale processing, and assessment of recovery circuits.\nFinancial services firm J.P. Morgan acted as financial adviser to Perpetua Resources in connection with the potential investment.\nThe U.S. Forest Service approved the mine plan for the Stibnite Gold Project in 2025. Perpetua expects a final investment decision in the first quarter of 2027. Perpetua projects average gold output of 463,000 oz. a year in the first four years and 296,000 oz. a year over the mine’s life. Probable reserves contain 4.8 million oz. of gold and 148.7 million lb. of antimony.\nUnder the non-binding memorandum, funding and supply rights remain optional. Any binding deal would require definitive written agreements, satisfactory due diligence and applicable approvals. Print\nTAG: critical minerals Idaho\nCOMMODITY: Gold Tungsten\nREGION: United States\nCOMPANY: Perpetua Resources\nRepublish this article",
